let us talk about timing because the

269
00:09:26,839 --> 00:09:31,080
rhythm of a bakery's day is completely

270
00:09:28,960 --> 00:09:32,880
unlike almost any other business. The

271
00:09:31,080 --> 00:09:35,280
vast majority of a bakery's daily

272
00:09:32,880 --> 00:09:38,440
revenue comes in a very narrow window.

273
00:09:35,280 --> 00:09:40,160
Roughly 65 to 80% of daily sales happen

274
00:09:38,440 --> 00:09:42,360
between 7:00 in the morning and 10:00 in

275
00:09:40,160 --> 00:09:44,240
the morning. That 3-hour stretch is when

276
00:09:42,360 --> 00:09:46,240
the commuters are moving, when people

277
00:09:44,240 --> 00:09:48,160
want their morning coffee and pastry,

278
00:09:46,240 --> 00:09:50,040
when the bread is fresh, and the display

279
00:09:48,160 --> 00:09:52,240
case is at its most impressive. For

280
00:09:50,040 --> 00:09:54,760
those 3 hours, a good bakery is running

281
00:09:52,240 --> 00:09:57,200
at full speed. Then 10:00 arrives.

282
00:09:54,760 --> 00:09:59,000
Traffic drops, sometimes dramatically.

283
00:09:57,200 --> 00:10:01,000
The afternoon is quiet, the evening is

284
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:02,560
quieter still. But here is the problem.

285
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:04,800
Rent does not pause between 10:00 and

286
00:10:02,560 --> 00:10:07,040
5:00. The ovens, refrigeration, and

287
00:10:04,800 --> 00:10:09,120
utilities keep drawing power. Staff who

288
00:10:07,040 --> 00:10:10,920
are scheduled until closing time still

289
00:10:09,120 --> 00:10:13,600
need to be paid whether they are serving

290
00:10:10,920 --> 00:10:15,440
100 customers an hour or five. All of

291
00:10:13,600 --> 00:10:17,200
the fixed costs of the business continue

292
00:10:15,440 --> 00:10:18,880
accumulating throughout the entire

293
00:10:17,200 --> 00:10:21,280
operating day, while revenue

294
00:10:18,880 --> 00:10:23,000
concentrates in a tiny fraction of it.

295
00:10:21,280 --> 00:10:24,880
This is why smart bakeries do not simply

296
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:26,880
wait for the next morning rush. They

297
00:10:24,880 --> 00:10:29,520
introduce afternoon products, lunch

298
00:10:26,880 --> 00:10:31,080
items, sandwiches, quiches, salads,

299
00:10:29,520 --> 00:10:33,160
savory pastries that give afternoon

300
00:10:31,080 --> 00:10:35,080
customers a reason to come in. They use

301
00:10:33,160 --> 00:10:37,120
the quiet hours in the kitchen to batch

302
00:10:35,080 --> 00:10:39,200
produce items for the next day or for

303
00:10:37,120 --> 00:10:41,000
wholesale orders. They extend their

304
00:10:39,200 --> 00:10:43,200
revenue window precisely because their

305
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:45,240
cost window never closes. And this is

306
00:10:43,200 --> 00:10:47,160
the moment to talk about coffee because

307
00:10:45,240 --> 00:10:49,000
coffee changes the economics of a bakery

308
00:10:47,160 --> 00:10:50,320
more than almost anything else. A

309
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:52,560
customer walks in for a loaf of

310
00:10:50,320 --> 00:10:55,160
sourdough at $9. That is one

311
00:10:52,560 --> 00:10:56,880
transaction. Now add a flat white. In a

312
00:10:55,160 --> 00:10:59,280
city like London or New York, that is

313
00:10:56,880 --> 00:11:01,800
another 450 to 550. The total

314
00:10:59,280 --> 00:11:03,720
transaction is now $14 instead of nine.

315
00:11:01,800 --> 00:11:05,600
But here is the important part. The cost

316
00:11:03,720 --> 00:11:06,960
of making that coffee is almost nothing

317
00:11:05,600 --> 00:11:09,960
compared to the cost of baking the

318
00:11:06,960 --> 00:11:12,160
bread. Coffee beans, milk, a paper cup.

319
00:11:09,960 --> 00:11:14,560
The raw input cost of a specialty latte

320
00:11:12,160 --> 00:11:17,480
might be 55 cents. The retail price is

321
00:11:14,560 --> 00:11:19,320
550. That is a gross margin of 90% on

322
00:11:17,480 --> 00:11:21,480
the coffee alone. Compare that to the

323
00:11:19,320 --> 00:11:24,160
sourdough loaf, which has a gross margin

324
00:11:21,480 --> 00:11:26,640
of around 85% but took hours of labor to

325
00:11:24,160 --> 00:11:29,040
produce. The coffee is faster to make,

326
00:11:26,640 --> 00:11:30,600
requires almost no kitchen footprint,

327
00:11:29,040 --> 00:11:32,360
and generates an extraordinary return

328
00:11:30,600 --> 00:11:34,480
per transaction. This is why you will

329
00:11:32,360 --> 00:11:36,440
notice that almost every serious bakery

330
00:11:34,480 --> 00:11:38,960
in America and Britain now operates a

331
00:11:36,440 --> 00:11:41,240
coffee program alongside their baking.

332
00:11:38,960 --> 00:11:43,360
It is not a sideline. It is a core part

333
00:11:41,240 --> 00:11:45,440
of the economics. Adding that $5

334
00:11:43,360 --> 00:11:48,400
beverage to a pastry sale can increase

335
00:11:45,440 --> 00:11:50,240
the value of a transaction by over 100%

336
00:11:48,400 --> 00:11:52,200
while adding almost no additional cost

337
00:11:50,240 --> 00:11:54,000
to the business. The product mix matters

338
00:11:52,200 --> 00:11:55,680
enormously in a bakery, which is

339
00:11:54,000 --> 00:11:57,360
something the display case never reveals

340
00:11:55,680 --> 00:11:59,480
to the customer, but the owner thinks

341
00:11:57,360 --> 00:12:01,600
about constantly. Not every product in

342
00:11:59,480 --> 00:12:03,280
the case earns the same return. Some

343
00:12:01,600 --> 00:12:06,040
items are there because customers expect

344
00:12:03,280 --> 00:12:08,400
them. A baguette, a plain croissant, a

345
00:12:06,040 --> 00:12:10,480
basic white loaf. These items may have

346
00:12:08,400 --> 00:12:12,600
fine ingredient costs, but they require

347
00:12:10,480 --> 00:12:14,760
skilled labor, they have high waste

348
00:12:12,600 --> 00:12:16,360
rates if unsold, and they tend to be low

349
00:12:14,760 --> 00:12:18,720
priced because customers have strong

350
00:12:16,360 --> 00:12:19,880
price expectations for them. Other items

351
00:12:18,720 --> 00:12:22,600
are there because they generate

352
00:12:19,880 --> 00:12:24,480
excellent returns. A specialty cake, a

353
00:12:22,600 --> 00:12:26,480
signature pastry that nobody else in the

354
00:12:24,480 --> 00:12:28,760
area makes, a seasonal item that

355
00:12:26,480 --> 00:12:31,000
commands a premium price. The bakery is

356
00:12:28,760 --> 00:12:32,760
not simply selling food. It is managing

357
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:34,520
a portfolio of products with wildly

358
00:12:32,760 --> 00:12:36,480
different economic profiles, and the

359
00:12:34,520 --> 00:12:38,600
decisions about what gets prime display

360
00:12:36,480 --> 00:12:40,839
space, what gets promoted, and what gets

361
00:12:38,600 --> 00:12:42,680
quietly retired have real consequences

362
00:12:40,839 --> 00:12:44,600
for the bottom line. There is also a

363
00:12:42,680 --> 00:12:46,080
concept called a loss leader, and it

364
00:12:44,600 --> 00:12:47,880
creates a specific trap worth

365
00:12:46,080 --> 00:12:50,080
understanding. A loss leader is a

366
00:12:47,880 --> 00:12:53,600
product priced very low on purpose. A

367
00:12:50,080 --> 00:12:55,200
$2.50 baguette, a $1.50 plain roll. The

368
00:12:53,600 --> 00:12:56,800
idea is that this cheap item brings

369
00:12:55,200 --> 00:12:58,960
customers through the door, and once

370
00:12:56,800 --> 00:13:01,720
inside they buy higher margin things. A

371
00:12:58,960 --> 00:13:02,920
coffee, a cake, a specialty pastry. If

372
00:13:01,720 --> 00:13:05,480
that is what actually happens, the

373
00:13:02,920 --> 00:13:06,960
strategy works. The low margin item pays

374
00:13:05,480 --> 00:13:08,880
for itself through the sales it drags

375
00:13:06,960 --> 00:13:10,640
along with it, but when a significant

376
00:13:08,880 --> 00:13:12,480
portion of customers buy only the cheap

377
00:13:10,640 --> 00:13:14,120
item and leave, you are running a busy

378
00:13:12,480 --> 00:13:17,400
operation that generates almost no

379
00:13:14,120 --> 00:13:18,720
profit. High volume, low return, maximum

380
00:13:17,400 --> 00:13:20,640
labor. Tracking whether your

381
00:13:18,720 --> 00:13:22,920
best-selling low margin product actually

382
00:13:20,640 --> 00:13:24,760
drives additional purchases or simply

383
00:13:22,920 --> 00:13:26,520
generates foot traffic that walks

384
00:13:24,760 --> 00:13:29,400
straight back out is one of the most

385
00:13:26,520 --> 00:13:31,280
valuable things a bakery owner can do.

386
00:13:29,400 --> 00:13:32,880
Now let us talk about custom cakes

387
00:13:31,280 --> 00:13:35,200
because they operate under a completely

388
00:13:32,880 --> 00:13:37,240
different set of economic rules. A

389
00:13:35,200 --> 00:13:38,760
custom celebration cake is not really a

390
00:13:37,240 --> 00:13:41,480
bakery product in the conventional

391
00:13:38,760 --> 00:13:43,040
sense. It is a skilled craft commission.

392
00:13:41,480 --> 00:13:44,520
When a customer orders a three-tier

393
00:13:43,040 --> 00:13:46,720
birthday cake with hand-piped

394
00:13:44,520 --> 00:13:48,560
decorations and a custom design, they

395
00:13:46,720 --> 00:13:50,200
are not primarily paying for butter and

396
00:13:48,560 --> 00:13:52,200
eggs. They are paying for the

397
00:13:50,200 --> 00:13:53,840
decorator's skill, for the hours of

398
00:13:52,200 --> 00:13:55,760
labor involved, for the design

399
00:13:53,840 --> 00:13:57,720
consultation, for the structural

400
00:13:55,760 --> 00:13:59,920
engineering that keeps a three-tier cake

401
00:13:57,720 --> 00:14:01,720
from collapsing, and for the emotional

402
00:13:59,920 --> 00:14:03,880
significance of a product that is being

403
00:14:01,720 --> 00:14:06,560
created specifically for one moment in

404
00:14:03,880 --> 00:14:08,000
one person's life. Many bakery owners,

405
00:14:06,560 --> 00:14:10,440
especially those who are new to the

406
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:12,160
business, price custom cakes using a

407
00:14:10,440 --> 00:14:13,600
simple formula. They calculate the

408
00:14:12,160 --> 00:14:16,400
ingredient cost and multiply it by

409
00:14:13,600 --> 00:14:19,520
three. If the ingredients cost $22, the

410
00:14:16,400 --> 00:14:21,320
cake sells for $66. This sounds logical.

411
00:14:19,520 --> 00:14:23,680
It is also wrong, and it routinely

412
00:14:21,320 --> 00:14:25,600
produces losses. A three-tier custom

413
00:14:23,680 --> 00:14:27,720
cake with hand-piped flowers and a

414
00:14:25,600 --> 00:14:30,120
custom inscription might use $22 in

415
00:14:27,720 --> 00:14:32,400
ingredients, but it also requires 5 and

416
00:14:30,120 --> 00:14:35,160
1/2 hours of a skilled decorator's time

417
00:14:32,400 --> 00:14:38,680
at a real wage of around $30 per hour.

418
00:14:35,160 --> 00:14:40,600
That is $165 in labor alone. Add kitchen

419
00:14:38,680 --> 00:14:42,720
overhead, the energy used, the

420
00:14:40,600 --> 00:14:44,400
specialist supplies, and the correct

421
00:14:42,720 --> 00:14:46,320
price for that cake, if the business

422
00:14:44,400 --> 00:14:49,120
wants to actually retain a profit, is

423
00:14:46,320 --> 00:14:52,640
somewhere north of $300. The ingredient

424
00:14:49,120 --> 00:14:54,440
* 3 formula would have priced it at $66.

425
00:14:52,640 --> 00:14:55,920
The difference between those two numbers

426
00:14:54,440 --> 00:14:58,400
is the difference between building a

427
00:14:55,920 --> 00:15:00,839
viable custom cake business and slowly

428
00:14:58,400 --> 00:15:02,760
hemorrhaging money on every order.

429
00:15:00,839 --> 00:15:05,240
Value-based pricing means charging what

430
00:15:02,760 --> 00:15:06,800
the skill, time, and emotional

431
00:15:05,240 --> 00:15:07,880
significance of the product is actually

432
00:15:06,800 --> 00:15:09,520
worth.

433
00:15:07,880 --> 00:15:11,960
And customers buying a custom wedding

434
00:15:09,520 --> 00:15:13,680
cake or a milestone birthday cake are

435
00:15:11,960 --> 00:15:15,080
generally willing to pay for it because

436
00:15:13,680 --> 00:15:17,200
they understand they are buying

437
00:15:15,080 --> 00:15:19,360
something irreplaceable. The bakeries

438
00:15:17,200 --> 00:15:21,200
that understand this price accordingly.

439
00:15:19,360 --> 00:15:23,240
The ones that do not are doing the most

440
00:15:21,200 --> 00:15:25,480
demanding work in the shop for the worst

441
00:15:23,240 --> 00:15:27,880
returns. There is also a category of

442
00:15:25,480 --> 00:15:29,160
hidden costs that only becomes visible

443
00:15:27,880 --> 00:15:31,000
once you are already running the

444
00:15:29,160 --> 00:15:32,680
business, and these costs tend to

445
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:34,800
surprise people who thought they had

446
00:15:32,680 --> 00:15:36,800
planned their finances carefully.

447
00:15:34,800 --> 00:15:38,880
Third-party delivery platforms. If a

448
00:15:36,800 --> 00:15:41,400
bakery offers delivery through services

449
00:15:38,880 --> 00:15:43,520
like DoorDash or Uber Eats or Deliveroo

450
00:15:41,400 --> 00:15:45,720
in the United Kingdom, those platforms

451
00:15:43,520 --> 00:15:48,640
charge commission rates between 15 and

452
00:15:45,720 --> 00:15:50,680
30% of every order value. On a $20

453
00:15:48,640 --> 00:15:52,560
order, the platform might take five or

454
00:15:50,680 --> 00:15:55,000
six dollars before a single ingredient

455
00:15:52,560 --> 00:15:57,200
cost or labor cost is considered. Many

456
00:15:55,000 --> 00:15:59,320
bakeries that rush to offer delivery

457
00:15:57,200 --> 00:16:00,920
without adjusting their online prices

458
00:15:59,320 --> 00:16:03,000
discover they are effectively paying

459
00:16:00,920 --> 00:16:05,080
customers to order from them rather than

460
00:16:03,000 --> 00:16:07,560
generating any profit at all. Payment

461
00:16:05,080 --> 00:16:09,760
processing on small transactions is also

462
00:16:07,560 --> 00:16:11,920
more expensive than most people realize.

463
00:16:09,760 --> 00:16:13,600
When someone pays by card for a $4.50

464
00:16:11,920 --> 00:16:16,800
croissant, the processing fee is

465
00:16:13,600 --> 00:16:18,800
typically around 10 cents plus 2.8% of

466
00:16:16,800 --> 00:16:21,680
the transaction. That works out to about

467
00:16:18,800 --> 00:16:24,000
23 cents on a $4.50 purchase. That is

468
00:16:21,680 --> 00:16:25,560
over 5% of the transaction value going

469
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:27,560
to the card network.

470
00:16:25,560 --> 00:16:29,839
Across thousands of small transactions

471
00:16:27,560 --> 00:16:31,280
per day, this cost becomes significant

472
00:16:29,839 --> 00:16:32,440
and is completely invisible to the

473
00:16:31,280 --> 00:16:34,200
customer.

474
00:16:32,440 --> 00:16:35,760
So, after working through all of this,

475
00:16:34,200 --> 00:16:38,240
what does the realistic financial

476
00:16:35,760 --> 00:16:39,839
picture of a bakery actually look like?

477
00:16:38,240 --> 00:16:42,040
Let us use what accountants call the

478
00:16:39,839 --> 00:16:44,000
break-even point. This is the daily

479
00:16:42,040 --> 00:16:45,760
sales level the bakery needs to reach

480
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:47,720
before it starts making any profit at

481
00:16:45,760 --> 00:16:50,120
all. Every dollar of sales before this

482
00:16:47,720 --> 00:16:51,800
point is simply paying fixed costs.

483
00:16:50,120 --> 00:16:54,080
Every dollar after this point is where

484
00:16:51,800 --> 00:16:56,200
the actual earnings begin. For our model

485
00:16:54,080 --> 00:16:58,920
bakery with $45,000

486
00:16:56,200 --> 00:17:00,680
in monthly revenue and roughly $14,000

487
00:16:58,920 --> 00:17:02,960
in fixed monthly overhead including

488
00:17:00,680 --> 00:17:05,319
rent, core payroll, insurance, and

489
00:17:02,960 --> 00:17:07,760
utilities, operating with a contribution

490
00:17:05,319 --> 00:17:09,839
margin of around 42%, the monthly

491
00:17:07,760 --> 00:17:11,600
break-even threshold works out to just

492
00:17:09,839 --> 00:17:14,319
over $33,000.

493
00:17:11,600 --> 00:17:16,439
That is about $1,100 in daily sales the

494
00:17:14,319 --> 00:17:18,560
bakery needs before a single dollar of

495
00:17:16,439 --> 00:17:20,680
profit is generated. On a typical

496
00:17:18,560 --> 00:17:22,920
operating day, the bakery needs to clear

497
00:17:20,680 --> 00:17:25,439
roughly the first 74% of its sales

498
00:17:22,920 --> 00:17:27,160
volume just to cover what it owes. Only

499
00:17:25,439 --> 00:17:29,280
the last quarter of the day's sales is

500
00:17:27,160 --> 00:17:31,640
generating actual margin. This is why

501
00:17:29,280 --> 00:17:33,480
small bakeries are so fragile. A bad

502
00:17:31,640 --> 00:17:35,880
week of weather, a street closure that

503
00:17:33,480 --> 00:17:37,400
blocks foot traffic for 3 days, a piece

504
00:17:35,880 --> 00:17:39,560
of equipment that breaks down and needs

505
00:17:37,400 --> 00:17:40,960
emergency repair. Any of these events

506
00:17:39,560 --> 00:17:42,800
can push daily sales below the

507
00:17:40,960 --> 00:17:45,480
break-even threshold and turn a

508
00:17:42,800 --> 00:17:47,440
marginally profitable week into a loss.

509
00:17:45,480 --> 00:17:49,640
The fixed costs never stop even when the

510
00:17:47,440 --> 00:17:51,320
register slows down. And now we arrive

511
00:17:49,640 --> 00:17:53,720
at the part of bakery economics that is

512
00:17:51,320 --> 00:17:55,720
almost never discussed publicly because

513
00:17:53,720 --> 00:17:57,720
it is uncomfortable. It involves the

514
00:17:55,720 --> 00:17:59,720
owner. There is a zone that many small

515
00:17:57,720 --> 00:18:01,680
bakery owners fall into and never

516
00:17:59,720 --> 00:18:03,360
escape. The business is busy enough to

517
00:18:01,680 --> 00:18:05,440
consume their entire life but not

518
00:18:03,360 --> 00:18:07,720
profitable enough to justify what they

519
00:18:05,440 --> 00:18:10,000
are sacrificing. The owner wakes up at

520
00:18:07,720 --> 00:18:11,360
4:00 in the morning to start baking.

521
00:18:10,000 --> 00:18:13,720
They serve behind the counter during the

522
00:18:11,360 --> 00:18:16,040
rush. They handle supplier calls in the

523
00:18:13,720 --> 00:18:17,720
afternoon, bookkeeping in the evening,

524
00:18:16,040 --> 00:18:19,400
social media whenever there is a spare

525
00:18:17,720 --> 00:18:22,320
minute. They clean the kitchen when

526
00:18:19,400 --> 00:18:24,000
staff go home. 70, sometimes 80 hours a

527
00:18:22,320 --> 00:18:26,320
week. And at the end of the year, the

528
00:18:24,000 --> 00:18:29,520
business shows a profit on paper. Maybe

529
00:18:26,320 --> 00:18:31,200
$40,000, maybe $60,000. But here is the

530
00:18:29,520 --> 00:18:32,960
question that matters. What would it

531
00:18:31,200 --> 00:18:35,600
cost to replace that owner with paid

532
00:18:32,960 --> 00:18:38,000
staff doing the same jobs? A head baker,

533
00:18:35,600 --> 00:18:40,160
a manager, a bookkeeper, a social media

534
00:18:38,000 --> 00:18:41,800
coordinator. When you add up the market

535
00:18:40,160 --> 00:18:43,800
wages for all the roles the owner is

536
00:18:41,800 --> 00:18:45,120
filling personally, the number is often

537
00:18:43,800 --> 00:18:47,120
higher than the profit the business

538
00:18:45,120 --> 00:18:49,640
generates, which means the business is

539
00:18:47,120 --> 00:18:51,640
not actually profitable. It is simply

540
00:18:49,640 --> 00:18:53,320
